3. the retired files 404
------------------------
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mathlab0911/arithmetic-landscapes/main/paper/paper3.tex
  empty -- not on main.

  The control matters here more than the observation: an empty body is what a
  broken fetcher returns too.  Item 2 is that control, and it returned 77 kB
  from the same directory over the same transport in the same minute.


4. the homepage
---------------
  https://mathlab0911.github.io/index.html
  200, and it serves the rewrite: r_A(n) first, the layer definition of Gamma,
  5.3492879..., three parts with current titles and statuses, a Use of AI
  tools section.

  https://mathlab0911.github.io/
  ** served the OLD page ** on the first fetch, roughly one minute after the
  push succeeded and while /index.html was already new: the enumeration
  definition, "order-sensitive invariant", four papers, 5.34920, no
  disclosure.  GitHub Pages had not finished rebuilding, or a cache had not
  expired.

  This is the finding of the round, and it is exactly why this check exists.
  Two green pushes, a green C18 over the local file, and the URL a reader
  actually types was still serving the sentence that C11 has banned since
  r118.  The bare URL is the one in the papers' author footnotes, in the
  README, and in the email that went to two mathematicians.

  Re-fetch of the bare URL is queued for r131; if it has not caught up, the
  cause is a cache and not the push, and that is a different repair.


5. second pass, in a real browser
---------------------------------
  The session fetcher would not re-read the bare URL, so this pass used a
  browser -- which is the reader's route anyway, and the honest way to answer
  the question item 4 left open.

  https://mathlab0911.github.io/   now serves the rewrite.  The stale read in
  item 4 was GitHub Pages' rebuild lag, roughly a minute, and not a defect in
  what was pushed.  The lesson survives the acquittal: for that minute the
  bare URL served a retracted sentence, and nothing in this repository could
  have told us.

  https://github.com/mathlab0911/arithmetic-landscapes  rendered, read as a
  reader reads it -- and reading it that way found three things that every
  check here had been walking past for rounds, because the README is inside
  the tree and was therefore assumed to be inside the checks:

    (a) The opening paragraph defined the ratio as lm_A(n)/deg_A(n).  deg_A
        was purged from all four papers at r126 for being a second name for
        r_A(n) -- the first of the three duplicate-name pairs the declined
        endorsement was about.  It survived in the first thing a reader sees.
        C9 counts the README's numbers; C14 guards one formula in it; neither
        has any opinion about its vocabulary.
    (b) "papers 2-4", after the series was renamed to Parts I, II, III.
    (c) The repository DESCRIPTION -- the one line under the repository name,
        and the browser tab title -- is still "The gap series of an integer
        sequence - an arithmetic invariant governing subset-sum landscapes",
        the pre-series title of Part I.  It is not in any file: it lives in
        GitHub's settings, so no check in any tree can reach it and no commit
        can fix it.  Left for Kentaro, flagged in the round report.

  (a) and (b) are fixed and C18 was widened to read the README the same way it
  reads the homepage -- with the mention-versus-claim distinction from r123,
  since the README must be able to name paper 3 in order to retire it.

  Two of the five negative controls for that exemption did not fire: a
  retirement note anywhere within 400 characters excused anything near it,
  including "four papers" reinstated three lines away.  That is C17's
  region-too-wide failure moved into the exemption, where it is worse -- a
  too-wide search only misses, a too-wide excuse forgives.  The marker must
  now stand in the same sentence as the name it retires.  All five controls
  fire.


what this check could not see
-----------------------------
  - The PDFs.  Only the .tex was fetched, and a reader of the release is more
    likely to open the PDF.
  - Anything that lives in GitHub's settings rather than in a file: the
    repository description, the topics, the social preview.  Item 5(c) is one
    of those, and this whole apparatus is structurally blind to them.
  - Whether the mathematics is correct.  Nothing here is about that.
